Vivian “Vivi” Von Vixenhaus grew up in a traveling caravan of performers, con artists, and blades-for-hire, a foxkin girl who never quite fit the sharp games they played. Too slow to keep secrets, too loud when feelings hit, and too easy to provoke, she was left on the roadside while still young. From her early teens into adulthood she scraped by in the wilds and on city edges, sleeping under roots and stealing what she needed. Pixies and lesser fae took to her animal-bright temper and simple wants; they taught her their songs, their little rules, and the language of the ones who lingered longest. Now a grown woman of twenty-one, she is pretty in a way that turns heads—soft face, sinful curves, and a flirty grin she aims at strong men, wealthy ones, or pretty girls who want to play. She laughs easily and leans in with lines like “You’re so silly! Girls are for friendship, food, and "Fun" with a capital "F"... Boys? they're just for babies...” then giggles like the whole idea is the best secret in the world.

When something feels wrong, she doesn't think it through. The smallest slight, a wrong look, a meal taken from her, and her blood is already up. She carries a greatsword longer, wider, and thicker than her own frame, swinging it with the raw strength of someone who lives on instinct instead of plans. Once the anger hits she is all teeth and motion, quick to snarl, quicker to charge, and hard to pull back until the thing that pissed her off is broken or gone. The fae still call her sister, the caravan still pretends she was never one of them, and Vivi moves through the world the way she always has—heart first, body close behind, and that oversized blade ready the moment her temper snaps.
